 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**No, not yet.** Inflation has picked back up in 2026 and is above the RBA's 2–3% target band.\n\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% year-on-year in August 2026, up from 1.9% in June 2025. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026 and eased to 3.5% in July before rising again.\n- **Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred underlying measure):** 3.6% in August 2026, up from 2.8% in June 2025. It has risen steadily since mid-2025 and has been flat at 3.6% for three months.\n- **Electricity:** Prices were down year-on-year through mid-2025, then jumped. The annual rise was 13.2% in August 2026, after 22.4% in June. This is a major driver of the headline figure, and the move reflects the base effect of last year's rebates as well as price increases.\n- **Rents:** Still rising but slowing. Annual growth was 3.6% in August 2026, down from 7.1% in June 2024.\n- **Policy response:** The RBA cut the cash rate to 3.60% by late 2025, then raised it from February 2026. It reached 4.35% in June 2026 and was 4.36% in September 2026. The rate is now higher than when inflation was last near target, which signals the Bank is worried about persistent price pressure.\n\n**Bottom line:** Inflation fell close to target in 2025 but has re-accelerated. Underlying inflation is 3.6%, above the target band, and the RBA has tightened in response. Whether it is \"under control\" depends on whether the trimmed mean starts to fall back toward 3% in the coming months, which the current data doesn't yet show. The latest figures are for August 2026, so the September quarter CPI may change this picture.",
